stributed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083342 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083342 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083466 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b5963314-027e-58a8-94c3-2b38175169a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1061 30927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "932c4862-d05e-5b90-85c8-d54c3a5130a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1061 30928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37a09439-27ba-50b5-9e58-473fafe4e323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1061 30929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd4c06eb-e846-5f38-b100-a7f13c969c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1061 30930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "828a2000-0cb2-577a-9a4c-50d319487a43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1061 37200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98535c60-8311-53a8-9f73-e175c3ec6497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is normally distributed. Then the probability density function $f(x)$ of $X$ is a unimodal, bell-shaped curve. Moreover, it is symmetrical about the mean, median, and mode, which all equal $\\mu$.  \n\n![](images/0c/0c8951446f52cea9db6c0a2b600fc53cb564c3d64ff0afab15c9756dc0c0f29b.png)\n\nWe can use the normal distribution to model a wide range of real-life situations because many real-world distributions are roughly bell-shaped and symmetrical around a mean value. \n\nPatients' blood pressures, people's weights, measurement errors, and IQ scores are just some of the real-world examples that can be modeled as a normal distribution. \n\nTo determine whether a given data is normal, the first thing to do is to check the shape of the distribution. For example, if a data set is roughly bell-shaped, then it *might* be normally distributed. \n\nIt should be noted that, in practice, statisticians will often need to perform several tests on the data to check for normality (these are called **normality tests**). We cannot rely only on a distribution's shape since other distributions are also bell-shaped. However, checking the shape is often a good starting point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1065 4966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9f150bce-c1b4-502c-bcb1-da067550b958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1080 "8.1%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "209a5dc2-51b8-5cf8-a3fd-432567049203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1080 "7.9%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "285a1d46-eca2-5392-87b2-9ae6a067d406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1080 "11.5%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "383eb090-4034-58c4-90ea-549e44d474a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1080 "9.6%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7be87196-7809-5fb8-a83b-62481b462d15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1080 "8.9%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc16961d-f939-5b46-a020-4871a6a9d710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186266502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0642bb25-91a1-5589-b640-3a5b72872474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1071 "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "763a853d-691e-5772-a9d4-a4d4d0b3eb60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268925 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1080 "0.51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ce3962b-9372-53e8-94dd-19aae1e9b687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1080 "0.10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8fb6f7f4-57a5-52f7-8e64-395b91ab0e51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "752c40b7-22f4-5848-b34a-2e00b4e7dbd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc161ec3-c1d9-5eb1-baaa-2ab67d18a5ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1071 "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6829b460-7921-509d-b187-5e5360672369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302161 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the rod length (in centimeters). Then we have $X∼N(100,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(96.8\\le X<102.4).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(96.8\\le X<102.4) & =P(\\frac{96.8-100}{4}\\le Z<\\frac{102.4-100}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60) & =P(Z<0.60)-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.60)-P(Z\\le -0.80) \\\\  & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.60) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.80) & =0.2119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80<Z<0.60) & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =0.7257-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.5138 \\\\  & ≈0.51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately $0.51.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1071 "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aace1ce7-1675-5a50-8ee4-f15b9647304c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302164 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
